Step-by-step: How to perform this task in Linux
Step 1: Check server uptime and load
uptime


Shows how long the server has been running and its load averages.
Step 2: Monitor CPU and processes
top


Check CPU utilization, memory consumption, and processes using excessive resources.

If installed, use:
mpstat 1 5


Step 3: Check memory usage
free -h


Review available memory and swap usage to identify potential memory pressure.
Step 4: Check disk space
df -hT


Identify filesystems approaching full capacity.

To check disk I/O performance, if sysstat is installed:
iostat -xz 1 5


Step 5: Check application and server services
For example, on RHEL or Rocky Linux:
systemctl status httpd
systemctl status sshd


On Ubuntu, the SSH service is usually named ssh:
systemctl status ssh


Replace httpd with your actual application service.
Step 6: Review errors and warnings
sudo journalctl -p warning --since today


To monitor new system log messages in real time:
sudo journalctl -f


Press Ctrl+C to stop following the logs.
Step 7: Check application availability
If the application uses HTTP or HTTPS, test its response:
curl -I http://localhost


For an HTTPS application:
curl -I https://your-application-domain


Replace the example domain with your actual application URL.
